# A snippet, not a site: the reverse_proxy of an app that runs on ronron and
# has a standby copy on the VPS. The `_` puts this file first in the import of
# managed/*.caddy: Caddy must read a snippet before a site imports it.
#
#   import failover <upstream on ronron> <standby on the VPS> <health path>
#
# - lb_policy first: each request goes to ronron while ronron is healthy. It
#   goes to the standby only when ronron is not healthy.
# - dial_timeout 2s: when ronron is off, WireGuard sends no error, and a new
#   connection waits for its timeout. A short timeout sends the request to the
#   standby after 2 seconds, not after a minute.
# - response_header_timeout 10s: Caddy keeps its connections to ronron open
#   between requests. When ronron goes off, a request on such a connection gets
#   no answer and no error. Without this timeout, it waits until Cloudflare
#   gives up (100 seconds). The apps answer in much less than 10 seconds.
# - lb_try_duration 12s: a request that fails on ronron goes to the standby in
#   the same request, during 12 seconds at most. It must be longer than
#   response_header_timeout. Caddy always retries a request that it could not
#   send. After a connection, it retries only a GET, so a POST never goes
#   twice.
# - fail_duration 30s: after a failed request, ronron stays out for 30 seconds
#   (passive health check).
# - health_uri: Caddy checks each upstream every 10 seconds (active health
#   check). ronron comes back into use after a good check, and 30 seconds
#   after its last failure at the earliest. The path must answer 200 without
#   work: a static file, or a cheap route.
(failover) {
	reverse_proxy {args[0]} {args[1]} {
		lb_policy first
		lb_try_duration 12s
		fail_duration 30s
		health_uri {args[2]}
		health_interval 10s
		health_timeout 3s
		transport http {
			dial_timeout 2s
			response_header_timeout 10s
		}
	}
}
